y i think beth was  really keen to put it to secure there  because  of course durham police when they did  look into dominic cummings's infamous  trip to barnard castle the wording was  quite woolly it sort of left him in the  gray zone a little bit because it said  that he might have breached the rules  but it said that if they had stopped him  they would have turned him round but not  necessarily fined him and they weren't  giving out retrospective fines anyway  now it's got to be a possibility that  that is one of the outcomes possible for  kierstarma and up until that point that  beth pinned him down at the end  he hadn't really been very clear about  what he would do in that circumstance i  think we now know what the threshold is  what the line is and that's been drawn  pretty clearly now  that it is a fixed penalty notice that  will trigger that resignation so if he  is issued with that fine then he will  step down as labour leader but if durham  police  find that he has potentially breached  the rules but don't issue with him with  a fine he will presumably carry on now  that could be the sort of worst of both  worlds because the whole point of making  this intervention today and setting this  out was as you heard him saying there to  try and sort of reclaim his mantle as a  man of integrity and put some distance  between him and boris johnson when it  comes to rule breaking um and sort of  try and chip away at the narrative that  has been pretty successfully advanced by  tory mps in the last few weeks that all  them all politicians are the same the  danger is if he gets found to have  potentially been in breach of the rules  but doesn't get a fixed penalty noticed  he'll stay in post but those attacks  will still keep coming  and there will still be potential for  his integrity his honor to be impugned  and the danger for kisakistama is he is  someone that trades off that integrity  very heavily as a politician it's one of  his big political pitches as a lea